  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1039 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nA fairly progressive upper level pattern will provide a variety of \nchanging weather conditions over the next week. A strong short wave \ntrough moving southeastward across the Intermountain West on \nSaturday will bring strong winds to the area. In response to the \nshort wave trough, much stronger surface cyclogenesis will develop \nin the lee of the Rockies on Saturday. As a result, strong westerly \nwinds will spread over the area. The strongest winds can be expected \nover the extreme southwestern Texas Panhandle and western South \nPlains where the surface pressure gradient will be strongest. \nDeepest boundary layer mixing will be located over this area where \nforecast mixing heights will extend up to 700mb. Winds at this level \nwill be increasing to 40-45kt on Saturday afternoon. Some high level \ncloud cover is anticipated which may dampen some of the mixing but \nis unclear on the extent of the cloud cover at the moment. The other \neffect will be in the form of much warmer temperatures from \ndownsloping winds. Forecast maximum temperatures for Saturday \nafternoon are on the order of 10 to 15 degrees above seasonal \naverages. This short wave will emerge onto the Central Plains \nSaturday evening then sending a cold front through the area. Strong \nwinds will not let up behind the front with possible advisory level \nwinds continuing through the overnight hours. A 1027mb surface ridge \nsettling over the area on Sunday will drastically drop temperatures \nwell below seasonal averages.\n\nAnother weaker short wave trough will move overhead on Monday again \nbringing breezy westerly surface winds and warmer temperatures. \nThere is still considerable uncertainty regarding a mid-week system \ntracking across the southwestern CONUS. The latest model runs have \ndropped the low farther southward which will provide increased \nchances of precipitation. However, there is still a chance of the \narea being within the dryslot of this system as it approaches the \nregion.",
